+[markdown cell tabs19_q2_heading]
+### Explain an Invalid Code
+
+```{index} Practice
+```
+
+
+[markdown cell tabs19_q2_task]
+A clerk initializes `charge` to zero, then assigns 8 for express and 3 for standard. Pickup also costs zero. The program leaves an unknown code unchanged and prints “Free pickup.”
+
+Explain why a plausible numeric result is not evidence that the code was recognized. Describe what the program must track or report before treating zero as a valid free service.
+
+<label for="practice-invalid-code">Your explanation</label>
+<textarea id="practice-invalid-code" data-practice-draft="invalid-code" rows="4" placeholder="Distinguish a valid zero charge from an unrecognized service."></textarea>
+
+```{dropdown} Hint
+Compare input `P` with input `X`. Both leave the same number in `charge`, but they do not describe the same service.
+```
+
+```{dropdown} Explanation
+Zero is a valid amount, not a recognition flag. An unknown code needs an explicit invalid branch, or a separate Boolean recording whether any supported case matched. Only a recognized code may produce the corresponding service report. A `switch` default can reject the code before printing a charge; an `if` chain can use a final invalid branch. Neither repair needs a loop or a user-defined method.
+```
+
